window.__hvCxRegister("crat", {"v":1,"dialogue":"crat","title":"Cratylus","speechLabel":"On this part of the dialogue","eras":[{"id":"early-empire","label":"Early Roman Empire"},{"id":"late-antiquity","label":"Late antiquity"},{"id":"byzantine","label":"Byzantium"},{"id":"renaissance","label":"Renaissance"},{"id":"modern","label":"Modern"}],"items":[{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":92,"ref":"","t":"The scope of the dialogue","x":"The scope of the Cratylus is to exhibit in things last the prolific energy of souls, and the assimilative power, which, essentially receiving, they evince through the rectitude of names. But since the partial energy of souls …","tier":"dialogue","r":[[1915,2204]],"rs":"the whole dialogue"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":99,"ref":"","t":"The persons, and names by nature or by convention","x":"That the persons of the dialogue are Cratylus the Heraclitean, of whom Plato was an auditor, who said that all names are from nature, and that such as are not from nature are not names, just as we say, that he who falsely …","tier":"passage","r":[[1915,1924]],"rs":"383a–384e"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":117,"ref":"","t":"True and false names; Protagoras and Euthydemus","x":"The reasoning of Hermogenes is as follows: If there is a transposition of names, names are from position, and are the symbols of things. But the first is true, and therefore the second. But the reasoning of Proclus is this: If …","tier":"passage","r":[[1925,1934]],"rs":"385a–386e"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":122,"ref":"","t":"Things and actions have a nature of their own","x":"That the power of the first infinity imparts from itself progression to all things whatever which are capable of subsistence; but bound, limits and circumscribes every thing, and establishes it in its proper boundaries. Thus in …","tier":"passage","r":[[1934,1938]],"rs":"386e–387d"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":127,"ref":"","t":"The name as an instrument: the shuttle","x":"If names, according to Aristotle, are from position, and are symbols of things and conceptions, it is necessary that the sentences composed from them, being enunciative from position, should not be said, from this very …","tier":"passage","r":[[1938,1945]],"rs":"387d–389a"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":134,"ref":"","t":"The legislator, the maker of names, and the dialectician","x":"It also appears to me that Plato establishes the legislator analogous to the demuirgus of the universe: for he it is, according to the Timæus, who establishes the laws of fate, and who governs all things conformably to law. …","tier":"passage","r":[[1943,1954]],"rs":"388d–390e"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":143,"ref":"","t":"Hermogenes sent to learn of the rectitude of names","x":"And Saturn it seems possesses with himself the highest causes of things collected and separated; through the celestial sections producing into parts the intellectual wholeness, and becoming the cause of generative progressions …","tier":"passage","r":[[1954,1957]],"rs":"390e–391c"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":149,"ref":"","t":"Homer's names of the gods","x":"Why does Plato eject from his Republic the poets about Homer, as imitators, but now introduces them as divine leaders of the rectitude of names? May we not say, that there the variety of imitation is unadapted to simple and …","tier":"passage","r":[[1957,1961]],"rs":"391c–392b"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":168,"ref":"","t":"Astyanax and Hector; how the founder of names looks to forms","x":"That with respect to the names Astyanax and Hector, the philosopher, looking to the form and the thing signified, calls them similar; but grammarians, directing their attention to the matter and the syllables, will say that they …","tier":"passage","r":[[1961,1974]],"rs":"392b–394e"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":172,"ref":"","t":"Theophilus, Mnesitheus; Orestes and Agamemnon","x":"That Socrates now appropriately introduces the words θεοφιλον and μνησιθεον, i. e. dear to divinity, and mindful of divinity; for the alliance of souls to divinity is effected through the love of a divine nature, and the …","tier":"passage","r":[[1974,1976]],"rs":"394e–395b"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":178,"ref":"","t":"Pelops","x":"That the particulars respecting Pelops teach us, first, to despise appearances, and to look to the whole periods of souls; and to be remiss with respect to human affairs, but strenuous with respect to virtue and things divine. …","tier":"passage","r":[[1977,1978]],"rs":"395c–395d"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":179,"ref":"","t":"Tantalus","x":"That the narration concerning Tantalus obscurely signifies a soul elevated through contemplation to the intelligible (for the intelligible is the nutriment of the gods); but falling from the intelligible place to earth, and …","tier":"passage","r":[[1978,1979]],"rs":"395d–395e"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":181,"ref":"","t":"Jupiter","x":"That Socrates from divine names, which are statues of the gods, recurs analytically to the powers and energies of the gods: for he leaves their essences, as being ineffable and unknown, to be alone surveyed by the flower of …","tier":"passage","r":[[1979,1981]],"rs":"395e–396b"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":198,"ref":"","t":"Saturn","x":"Why does Socrates apprehend the name of king Saturn to be υβριστικον, insolent, and looking to what does he assert this? We reply, that according to the poets satiety (κορος) is the cause of insolence; for they thus denominate …","tier":"passage","r":[[1981,1981]],"rs":"396b"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":210,"ref":"","t":"Heaven","x":"That Heaven, the father of Saturn, is an intellect, understanding himself indeed, but united to the first intelligibles; in which he is also firmly established; and connectedly contains all the intellectual orders, by abiding in …","tier":"passage","r":[[1981,1982]],"rs":"396b–396c"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":216,"ref":"","t":"Orpheus and Hesiod; the inspiration of Euthyphro","x":"That Orpheus greatly availed himself of the license of fables, and manifests every thing prior to Heaven by names, as far as to the first cause. He also denominates the ineffable, who transcends the intelligible unities, Time …","tier":"passage","r":[[1982,1985]],"rs":"396c–397a"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":227,"ref":"","t":"Gods and daemons","x":"That many dæmons have thought fit to unfold the nature of the gods, and have also delivered names adapted to the gods. Thus, too, the gods themselves not only unfolded the intelligible and intellectual orders to the theurgists …","tier":"passage","r":[[1987,1992]],"rs":"397c–398c"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":236,"ref":"","t":"Daemons and heroes","x":"That Plato now speculates about dæmons and heroes, not those which subsist according to habitude, but those which are beyond our essence. He recurs however, through analogy, from those which subsist according to habitude, to …","tier":"passage","r":[[1989,1994]],"rs":"397e–398e"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":222,"ref":"","t":"Heroes","x":"But how is it that at one time the gods are said to have connexion with mortal females, and at another time mortal females with the gods. We reply that the communion of gods with goddesses gives subsistence to gods, or dæmons …","tier":"passage","r":[[1992,1994]],"rs":"398c–398e"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":238,"ref":"","t":"Man (anthropos)","x":"That syllables and letters appear to have the relation of essence in names, but acute and grave accents are certain powers of names.","tier":"passage","r":[[1995,1997]],"rs":"399a–399c"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":239,"ref":"","t":"Soul and body","x":"That the intellect in us is Dionysiacal, and is the true image of Bacchus. He therefore who acts erroneously with respect to it, and Titannically divulses its impartible nature through manifold falsehood, certainly sins against …","tier":"passage","r":[[1998,2002]],"rs":"399d–400c"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":240,"ref":"","t":"What we may say of the gods' names","x":"That we are more able to understand the more total genera of the gods, than such as are more partial. For in the leading and ruling gods, we can obtain a clearer knowledge of that which is total in their subsistence, and extends …","tier":"passage","r":[[2003,2005]],"rs":"400d–401a"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":244,"ref":"","t":"Vesta","x":"For in prayers they are called upon to celebrate Vesta before the other gods.","tier":"passage","r":[[2006,2009]],"rs":"401b–401e"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":248,"ref":"","t":"Rhea and Saturn","x":"That the theology of Hesiod from the monad Rhea produces, according to things which are more excellent in the coordination, Vesta; but, according to those which are subordinate, Juno; and according to those which subsist between …","tier":"passage","r":[[2009,2012]],"rs":"401e–402c"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":254,"ref":"","t":"Ocean and Tethys","x":"That Ocean is the cause to all the gods of acute and vigorous energy, and bounds the separations of the first, middle, and last orders; converting himself to himself, and to his proper principles, through swiftness of intellect …","tier":"passage","r":[[2011,2013]],"rs":"402b–402d"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":258,"ref":"","t":"Neptune, and the demiurgic triad","x":"That Saturn is the monad of the Titannic order of the gods, but Jupiter of the demiurgic. This last divinity however is two-fold, the one exempt and coordinated with Saturn, being a fontal god, and, in short, ranking with the …","tier":"passage","r":[[2013,2015]],"rs":"402d–403a"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":269,"ref":"","t":"Pluto","x":"That some badly analyze the name of Pluto into wealth from the earth, through fruits and metals; but Hades into the invisible, dark, and dreadful. These Socrates now reprobates, bringing the two names to the same signification …","tier":"passage","r":[[2015,2021]],"rs":"403a–404b"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":281,"ref":"","t":"Ceres","x":"That, according to Orpheus, Ceres is the same with Rhea: for Orpheus says, that subsisting on high in unproceeding union with Saturn, she is Rhea, but that by emitting and generating Jupiter, she is Ceres. For thus he speaks,","tier":"passage","r":[[2021,2021]],"rs":"404b"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":290,"ref":"","t":"Juno","x":"That the series of our sovereign mistress Juno beginning from on high pervades to the last of things; and her allotment in the sublunary region is the air. For air is a symbol of soul, according to which also soul is called a …","tier":"passage","r":[[2021,2022]],"rs":"404b–404c"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":294,"ref":"","t":"Proserpine","x":"That a lover of piety to the gods ought earnestly to embrace the rectitude of divine names, lest, like those who err concerning Proserpine and Apollo, being ignorant of the analysis of names, he should be subject to the same …","tier":"passage","r":[[2022,2023]],"rs":"404c–404d"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":296,"ref":"","t":"Apollo","x":"That very rationally after Proserpine, Plato now analyzes Apollo: for there is a great communion between the Coric and the Apolloniacal series; since the former is the unity of the middle triad of rulers (i. e. of the …","tier":"passage","r":[[2023,2030]],"rs":"404d–406a"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":311,"ref":"","t":"The Muses","x":"From discoursing about king Apollo, Plato proceeds to the Muses, and the name of music: for Apollo is celebrated as Musagetes; and he indeed is a monad with respect to the harmony in the world, but the choir of the Muses is the …","tier":"passage","r":[[2030,2030]],"rs":"406a"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":313,"ref":"","t":"Latona","x":"That Latona is a vivific fountain comprehended in Ceres: and hence, according to the Grecian rites, she is worshipped as the same with Ceres, these rites evincing by this the union of the goddesses. But this goddess emits the …","tier":"passage","r":[[2030,2030]],"rs":"406a"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":315,"ref":"","t":"Diana","x":"That of our sovereign mistress Diana, Plato delivers three idioms; the undefiled, the mundane, and the anagogic. And through the first of these indeed the goddess is said to be a lover of virginity; but through the second …","tier":"passage","r":[[2031,2031]],"rs":"406b"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":318,"ref":"","t":"Bacchus","x":"That Plato coarranges the mundane Bacchus with the mundane Venus, in consequence of her love of Bacchus, and her fashioning, as an image of him, Adonis much honoured in Cilicia and Cyprus. And it is evident that a love of this …","tier":"passage","r":[[2031,2032]],"rs":"406b–406c"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":325,"ref":"","t":"Venus","x":"They say then that the first Venus was produced from two-fold causes, the one as that through which, cooperating with her progression, as calling forth the prolific power of the father, and imparting it to the intellectual …","tier":"passage","r":[[2032,2033]],"rs":"406c–406d"},{"c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":326,"ref":"","t":"Minerva","x":"That theologists especially celebrate two powers of our sovereign mistress Minerva, the defensive and the perfective, the former preserving the order of wholes undefiled, and unvanquished by matter, and the other filling all …","tier":"passage","r":[[2033,2037]],"rs":"406d–407c"},{"c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":222,"ref":"","t":"Introduction to the Cratylus","x":"The ensuing Dialogue, which disputes whether names have been assigned to things from nature or position, and whether some at least are not derived from a more divine origin than that of human invention, has been highly censured …","tier":"dialogue","r":[[1915,2204]],"rs":"the whole dialogue"}],"rk":"crat","glosses":[{"s":1915,"ri":1,"ph":"If you wish.","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":103,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes that Cratylus, as a Heraclitean, uses the greatest brevity, answering throughout in the fewest words, since speech cannot keep pace with the flowing nature of things.","q":"That Cratylus being scientific, and employing the greatest brevity of diction, which was the peculiarity of the Heraclitics, in consequence of enunciations not being able to keep pace with the flowing nature of things, appears to answer, through the whole of the dialogue, from the fewest syllables and words."},{"s":1915,"ri":2,"ph":"There is, rather, a natural correctness of names","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":99,"ref":"","n":"Proclus identifies Cratylus as the Heraclitean whom Plato heard, who held that all names are from nature and that names not from nature are no names at all.","q":"That the persons of the dialogue are Cratylus the Heraclitean, of whom Plato was an auditor, who said that all names are from nature, and that such as are not from nature are not names"},{"s":1915,"ri":2,"ph":"a correctness of names that belongs, by nature, to each of the things that are","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":224,"ref":"","n":"Taylor identifies Cratylus' view with the Heraclitean doctrine that names exist by nature alone, the consent of men contributing nothing to their formation.","q":"And the Heraclitics indeed asserted that names consist from nature alone, and that the consent of men contributes nothing to their formation or invention."},{"s":1916,"ri":2,"ph":"his own name is, in truth, Cratylus","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":108,"ref":"","n":"Proclus derives the name Cratylus from his firm hold on the doctrines of Heraclitus, and Socrates from his being saviour of the strength of his soul, that is, his reason.","q":"That the name Cratylus appears to have been assigned παρα τῳ περικρατησαι αραροτως των ηρακλειτου δογματων, from firmly obtaining the dog- mas of Heraclitus"},{"s":1918,"ri":2,"ph":"pretending to be in possession of some personal understanding of his own","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":111,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes that Hermogenes here, like Theodorus in the Theaetetus, accuses the Heracliteans of arrogance and dissembling, but observes that neither accuser is a philosopher.","q":"That the Heraclitics are accused as arrogant, as dissemblers, and as despisers, by Theodorus in the Theætetus, and now by Hermogenes."},{"s":1920,"ri":3,"ph":"there is an ancient saying that whatever is noble is difficult to understand","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":112,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes Socrates' use of the proverb to show he did not think the inquiry into the rectitude of names should be despised.","q":"That Socrates did not think that the speculation about the rectitude of names was to be despised, but according to the proverb he considered fine things to be difficult."},{"s":1921,"ri":3,"ph":"every time you set out to make some money, you fail","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":115,"ref":"","n":"Proclus lists the gifts of Hermes in descending rank, the last being externally proceeding powers and gain, the material gifts the god imparts in ignoble dispositions.","q":"Others again give subsistence to the reasons of nature; and others are the suppliers of externally proceeding powers, and of gain. For these are the last and the material gifts of the god, which, as astrologers say, the god imparts in ignoble dispositions (αδοξοις διαθεσεσιν)."},{"s":1922,"ri":4,"ph":"any correctness of a name apart from convention and agreement","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":99,"ref":"","n":"Proclus contrasts Hermogenes, who held all names to be from position, with Socrates, who distinguishes: names of perpetual things rather come from nature, those of corruptible things rather from chance.","q":"For the names which belong to things perpetual, rather participate of a subsistence from nature, but those which belong to things corruptible, rather partake of the casual."},{"s":1922,"ri":4,"ph":"any correctness of a name apart from convention and agreement","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":227,"ref":"","n":"Taylor, following Ammonius, classes Hermogenes as a Parmenidean who held that names may be formed by anyone's arbitrary choice, even without any rational cause.","q":"And one part indeed defended such a position of names, as the Parmenidæan Hermogenes in the present Dialogue, viz. that names might be formed according to every one’s arbitrary determination, though this should take place without any rational cause:"},{"s":1925,"ri":9,"ph":"suppose I refer to this as a horse","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":117,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reports Hermogenes' argument that because names can be transposed they are from position, and turns it round: if names were mere symbols by position, there would be no need to transpose them.","q":"The reasoning of Hermogenes is as follows: If there is a transposition of names, names are from position, and are the symbols of things."},{"s":1926,"ri":17,"ph":"is it possible in a statement to state things that are and things that are not?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":119,"ref":"","n":"Against Antisthenes' claim that nothing can be contradicted, Proclus answers that a man speaking of being may still speak falsely, since he speaks about something rather than speaking something.","q":"In answer to this we must say, that there is also that which is false, and that nothing hinders but that the man who speaks of being may speak falsely."},{"s":1927,"ri":31,"ph":"is it also possible to utter a true and a false name?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":118,"ref":"","n":"Proclus distinguishes Plato's truth of names from Aristotle's truth of sentences: Aristotle means composition and division of predicates, while Plato says names essentially predicated are true.","q":"That the truth of an enunciative sentence (του απφοαντικου λογου), means one thing with Aristotle, and another with Plato in the present place, in which he says, that names essentially predicated (καθ αυτα λεγομενα) are true."},{"s":1927,"ri":31,"ph":"a true and a false name","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":118,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Plato uses true and false in four ways, of things, passions, knowledge, and instruments of knowing such as names, which are true or false by their fit with things.","q":"Or according to the instruments of the gnostic life, as, for instance, assertions, names, and elements. For in these the true and the false are seen, according to their adaptation and symphony with things."},{"s":1929,"ri":37,"ph":"the measure of all things is man","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":121,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains Protagoras' doctrine: though the subject has no existence, it appears to beholders to have a certain quality through the commixture of agent and patient.","q":"For the former says, that though the subject has no existence, yet it appears to beholders to possess a particular quality, through the commixture of the agent and patient."},{"s":1931,"ri":45,"ph":"Are those who are very good also very wise, while the very bad are very foolish?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":120,"ref":"","n":"Proclus adds that the good know the bad better than the bad know the virtue of the good, for vice is blind, ignorant first of itself and then of others.","q":"That bad are more known by good men, than the virtue of good men is by the bad. For vice is blind; and in the first place is ignorant of itself, and in the next place of others."},{"s":1933,"ri":51,"ph":"the opinion of Euthydemus","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":121,"ref":"","n":"Proclus distinguishes Euthydemus' doctrine, that everything is all things and all things are always true together, from Protagoras', though both sophists end in the same place.","q":"But the dogma of Euthydemus makes every thing to be all things, and asserts that all things are at the same time always true."},{"s":1933,"ri":53,"ph":"things are possessed of some definite being of their own","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":122,"ref":"","n":"Proclus grounds this in the first bound, which limits each thing and gives it its own nature, idiom and order; hence contradiction in words can distinguish the false from the true.","q":"so that every being has a certain nature, bound, idiom, and proper order, through the first bound. There is therefore contradiction in words, which definitely exhibits the false and the true."},{"s":1933,"ri":53,"ph":"things are possessed of some definite being of their own","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":228,"ref":"","n":"Taylor takes this as Socrates' refutation of Hermogenes: names cannot rest on arbitrary choice alone, because there are universal genera and immutable natures to which a stable rightness of names belongs.","q":"For he plainly demonstrates that names cannot alone consist from the arbitrary determination of men, as Hermogenes seemed to assert, on account of the universal genera of things, and immutable and eternal natures to which a stable and right reason of names may be well ascribed,"},{"s":1934,"ri":55,"ph":"the actions belonging to the things","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":123,"ref":"","n":"Proclus distinguishes acting, said of those who energise by the dianoetic power, from making, and holds that actions and makings have their proper boundaries, instruments and times.","q":"Actions therefore and makings have their proper boundaries, instruments, and times; nor does any casual thing do or act any casual thing."},{"s":1936,"ri":63,"ph":"speaking is one of these activities, is it not?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":124,"ref":"","n":"Proclus proves that speech falls under action by division: energies of soul with body are involuntary or voluntary, and voluntary ones are action, under which discourse is ranked.","q":"or it is attended with free will, and this is action, under which is discourse."},{"s":1939,"ri":77,"ph":"whatever has to be bored have to be bored with something","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":131,"ref":"","n":"Proclus finds the shuttle and the borer fitting paradigms of a name: like a shuttle it separates things, and like a borer it enters the learner through the depth of his conceptions.","q":"That a shuttle and an augur are paradigms adapted to a name. For a name separates things from each other, and enters into the learner, through the depth of his conceptions."},{"s":1940,"ri":87,"ph":"So the name is also an instrument.","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":128,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reasons that whoever names acts through an instrument, and a name is an artificial instrument for signifying and teaching, which unfolds the essence of things from its paradigm.","q":"A name therefore is a thing of this kind: for it is an instrument which teaches and unfolds into light the essence of things; the teaching being assumed from him who uses the instrument, but the unfolding into light from the paradigm."},{"s":1940,"ri":87,"ph":"the name is also an instrument","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":128,"ref":"","n":"Proclus infers that a name is no mere symbol of chance position: every instrument is coordinated to its proper work, so a name has a connate power harmonising with what it signifies.","q":"So that it is evident from these things, that a name is not a symbol, nor the work of any casual position, but is allied to things, and adapted to them by nature."},{"s":1941,"ri":91,"ph":"Don’t we separate the commingled warp and woof threads?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":139,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes the shuttle as an image of the separating power of the gods; theologians use it symbolically by analogy, as a signature of separating power, as the cup signifies vivific power.","q":"That the shuttle is an image of the separating power of the gods, both of those that preside over wholes, and those that preside over parts; for its operation in woofs represents the energy of this power"},{"s":1942,"ri":99,"ph":"a name is an instrument of instruction and it divides being","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":130,"ref":"","n":"Proclus calls a name a doctrinal instrument, an image of things that announces them through conceptions, and a medium between teacher and learner.","q":"Very properly therefore is a name said to be a doctrinal instrument , οργανον διδασκαλικον"},{"s":1943,"ri":117,"ph":"Don’t you think that convention has given these to us?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":132,"ref":"","n":"Proclus argues names come from both law and nature, law here being eternal, not casual: through their scientific producing cause they are from law and position, through their paradigm from nature.","q":"A name therefore, through its producing cause, which is scientific, is both from law and position; but through its paradigmatic cause is from nature."},{"s":1945,"ri":123,"ph":"some fashioner of names who is, it seems, the convention-setter","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":133,"ref":"","n":"Proclus describes the art of the name-giver as the soul's assimilative power, which, like the telestic art making statues of gods, produces names as statues of things.","q":"so the legislative art, according to the same assimilative power, gives subsistence to names, the statues of things; through such and such sounds shadowing forth the nature of things, and having given subsistence to them, delivers them to the use of mankind."},{"s":1945,"ri":123,"ph":"the rarest of artificers to arise among us humans","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":134,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Plato makes the legislator analogous to the demiurge of the universe, who in the Timaeus named the circles same and different; hence he calls him a demiurge, the rarest of demiurges.","q":"Hence in this Dialogue Plato calls the legislator, demiurgus , and the most rare of demiurgi."},{"s":1945,"ri":123,"ph":"some fashioner of names who is, it seems, the convention-setter","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":227,"ref":"","n":"Taylor reports the older Peripatetic view, which he holds agrees with Plato's, that the artificer of names must possess universal science to fashion fitting names for all things.","q":"And that the artificer of names ought to be a person endued with universal science, in order that he may be able to fabricate proper and becoming names for all the variety of things."},{"s":1946,"ri":127,"ph":"to that form towards which he was looking when he made the one that broke","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":136,"ref":"","n":"Proclus denies that there are ideas of artefacts: the carpenter looks not to an intelligible but to the form of a shuttle in his own mind, first conceived by its inventor from its use.","q":"For the first artificer of a shuttle conceived in his own mind what kind of a thing a shuttle ought to be, looking to its use, and being led by this, produced in himself that form of a shuttle according to which others are made."},{"s":1946,"ri":127,"ph":"If the shuttle breaks while he is making it","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":137,"ref":"","n":"Proclus sees here an image of the demiurgic art: corruption comes from matter, renewal from the stable form, so shuttles derive from opinion-level forms, names from scientific forms, sensibles from intellectual forms.","q":"Just as in the shuttles which are here, the corruption is from the matter, but the regeneration from the reason or productive principle in the artist."},{"s":1948,"ri":139,"ph":"make and impose all names by looking to what name itself is","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":135,"ref":"","n":"Proclus compares the name-giver to the demiurge, whose assimilative energy is twofold: making the world by looking to an intelligible paradigm, and promulgating names adapted to things.","q":"Thus therefore the legislator also, looking to the whole world, delivers the most excellent polity, and imposes names assimilated to things."},{"s":1951,"ri":144,"ph":"The user, Socrates, is more likely to know this.","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":141,"ref":"","n":"Proclus raises a difficulty: if the user is better than the maker, how does a partial soul use the body made by the junior gods? He answers that the soul too contributes to its fabrication.","q":"If he who uses an instrument is better than him who fabricates it as being more architectonic, how does a partial soul use the irrational nature, and the shelly body, which were fabricated by the junior gods?"},{"s":1953,"ri":161,"ph":"with the dialectical person as supervisor","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":142,"ref":"","n":"Proclus resolves why the dialectician ranks above the legislator: he uses the legislator's work as a principle, not an instrument, and is analogous to Saturn, while the legislator answers to Jupiter.","q":"Hence the legislator is analogous to the demiurgus Jupiter, inserting the laws of fate in souls, and promulgating names to the whole circulations. But he who is skilled in dialectic, is analogous to the Saturnian monad."},{"s":1955,"ri":165,"ph":"I don’t know about this, but I could enquire along with you","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":145,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains that the philosopher's intellect, being an illumination and image of intellect rather than essential intellect, understands divisibly and sometimes only touches on the truth.","q":"but the intellect of the philosopher not being essential, but an illumination, and, in short, an image of intellect, understands divisibly, and sometimes only touches on the truth."},{"s":1956,"ri":167,"ph":"if you have a desire to know, the next thing we should do is to investigate","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":146,"ref":"","n":"Proclus lists five habits of men towards knowledge, from twofold ignorance through simple ignorance to desire, investigation and invention.","q":"That there are five habits of men with respect to knowledge, viz. twofold ignorance, simple ignorance, desire, investigation, invention."},{"s":1956,"ri":168,"ph":"But of course I want to know.","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":148,"ref":"","n":"Proclus makes Socrates analogous to intellect and Hermogenes to irrational opinion aspiring after the good, while Cratylus answers to corporeal phantasy and is deceived by the sophists.","q":"That Socrates is analogous to intellect; but Hermogenes to irrational opinion, aspiring after good; and Cratylus is analogous to the corporeal and material phantasy"},{"s":1956,"ri":171,"ph":"The correct way to enquire, my friend, is in the company of those who know","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":147,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says that one who scientifically knows the methods of discovery interprets them to the learner, in imitation of the leading Hermes.","q":"That he who has a scientific knowledge of the methods of invention interprets to the learner, imitating the leading Hermes."},{"s":1958,"ri":173,"ph":"you should learn from Homer and the other poets","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":149,"ref":"","n":"Proclus asks why Plato expels Homer and the poets from the Republic as imitators yet here makes them guides to the correctness of names, and answers that he rejects their imitation but embraces their inspired conceptions.","q":"May we not say, that there the variety of imitation is unadapted to simple and unperverted manners; but that here and every where he admires and embraces their divinely-inspired conceptions?"},{"s":1958,"ri":175,"ph":"the names that humans and the gods call the same things","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":157,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains the double naming: poets possessed by Phoebus ascribe some names to the gods, drawn from inspiration, and others to men, drawn from sense and opinion.","q":"Thus too poets inspired by Phœbus (των ποιητων οι φοιβοληπτοι) ascribe many names to the gods, and to human names give a division opposite to these; receiving the former from enthusiastic energy, and the latter from sense and opinion;"},{"s":1958,"ri":175,"ph":"the gods call things by names that are correct by nature","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":164,"ref":"","n":"Proclus holds that the names by which the gods are said to call things, as handed down by theologians, really come from the gods and not merely from daemons, as the mysteries show.","q":"That the names delivered by theologists by which the gods call things, are from the gods, and not from dæmons only."},{"s":1958,"ri":175,"ph":"he makes significant, fine distinctions","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":158,"ref":"","n":"Proclus finds in the three pairs of names three contrasts between divine and human knowledge: efficacious and passive, logical and physical, harmonious and unharmonious.","q":"In these too, we have the three-fold differences of divine and human knowledge; the efficaciou s and passive, in Xanthus and Scamander; the logical and physical, in Myrine and Batieia; and the enharmonic and unharmonic, in Chalcis and Cymindis."},{"s":1959,"ri":177,"ph":"is called Xanthos by the gods, but Scamander by humans","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":158,"ref":"","n":"Proclus observes that the divine names in these pairs are smoother, better sounding and shorter than the human ones, as Xanthus is compared with Scamander.","q":"That the names which are assigned to things by the gods are smooth, well-sounding, and of fewer syllables, than those which are assigned by men, as, for instance, Xanthus than Scamander, Chalcis than Cymindis, and Myrine than Batieia."},{"s":1959,"ri":177,"ph":"Xanthos","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":158,"ref":"","n":"Proclus cites Aristotle that cattle drinking from the river grew yellower, and suggests that the gods, knowing the causes of things, named the river Xanthus (yellow) for this reason.","q":"And with respect to Xanthus, Aristotle relates, that the skin of the cattle that drank out of it was yellower than before; and on this account perhaps the gods, who both produce and know the causes of all things, thus denominate it."},{"s":1959,"ri":177,"ph":"Scamander by humans","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":158,"ref":"","n":"Proclus suggests that men of superficial understanding called the river Scamander from the visible fact that its water runs through a channel dug by hand.","q":"But the apparent cause of its appellation perhaps was this, that its water passes through a drain made by the hand (οτι δια τινος χειροποιητου σκαφης διερχεται το υδωρ αυτου); and thus by men of superficial conceptions was called Scamander."},{"s":1960,"ri":180,"ph":"the gods have called Chalcis but humans call Cymnidis","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":158,"ref":"","n":"Proclus derives the divine name Chalcis from the bird's shrill, brazen sound, a name the Chaldaeans also use, and the human name Cymindis from the bird's leaping.","q":"Chalcis, perhaps, was so called from the shrill and canorous, like sharp sounding brass; for thus certainly the Chaldæans call it, having heard this name from the gods. But Cymindis is from the leaping of the bird (παρα την σκιρτοτητα του ορνεου);"},{"s":1960,"ri":180,"ph":"the hill that is called both Batieia and Myrina","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":158,"ref":"","n":"Proclus derives the divine name Myrine from the soul to whom the gods allotted that place, and the human name Batieia from the plant that grows abundantly there.","q":"and Myrine, from the soul allotted that place from the gods. Lastly, Batieia perhaps was thus called, through the plant which abounds in it."},{"s":1966,"ri":203,"ph":"If a horse, contrary to nature, were to give birth to a calf","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":170,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains such a birth: the particular nature is overcome, so no horse results, but universal nature prevails, so an ox is still generated.","q":"For when an ox is generated from an horse, the partial nature is subdued, and on this account a horse is not generated; but whole nature vanquishes, and on this account an ox is generated."},{"s":1966,"ri":203,"ph":"a sort of aberration","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":170,"ref":"","n":"Proclus remarks that Plato does not simply call such births portents (terata) but only something like portents, since they are not wholly contrary to nature.","q":"Hence Plato does not now simply call things of this kind, τερατα, but as it were τιρατα, as not being entirely contrary to nature."},{"s":1967,"ri":205,"ph":"the essence of the thing is in control and is revealed in the name","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":169,"ref":"","n":"Proclus draws the principle that the founder of names must look to the forms of the things named, illustrated by souls that keep the names of their leading gods.","q":"That it is necessary the founder of names should establish names looking to the forms of the things denominated."},{"s":1971,"ri":209,"ph":"'Astyanax' and 'Hector' have no letters the same except 't', but they indicate the same thing","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":168,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes that the philosopher, looking to the form and the thing signified, calls Astyanax and Hector similar, whereas grammarians, attending to matter and syllables, call them dissimilar.","q":"That with respect to the names Astyanax and Hector, the philosopher, looking to the form and the thing signified, calls them similar; but grammarians, directing their attention to the matter and the syllables, will say that they are dissimilar."},{"s":1971,"ri":209,"ph":"is not misled if a letter is added, transposed or removed","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":171,"ref":"","n":"Proclus lists what the etymologist must know, including dialects, poetic usage, compounds, and the alterations of words such as apocope, syncope, ellipsis and pleonasm.","q":"In the sixth place, he ought to know the properties of words, such as apocopies, syncopies, ellipses, pleonasms, and the like."},{"s":1974,"ri":218,"ph":"'Theophilus' (beloved of God) or 'Mnēsitheos' (mindful of God)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":172,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Socrates fittingly chooses these names because souls are allied to the divine through love of it and recollection of it, and only such souls deserve paternal, intellectual names.","q":"That Socrates now appropriately introduces the words θεοφιλον and μνησιθεον, i. e. dear to divinity, and mindful of divinity; for the alliance of souls to divinity is effected through the love of a divine nature, and the recollection of the hyparxis of deity;"},{"s":1974,"ri":220,"ph":"whether it was given to him by chance","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":173,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes chance here as fortune, a cause guided by intellect: fathers name children from memory or hope, but fortune names them in harmony with their whole lives.","q":"For fathers, looking to memory or hope, or something of this kind, give names to their children; but fortune gives them names after another manner, through a symphony with their lives."},{"s":1974,"ri":220,"ph":"by a poet to point out with this name the wildness","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":173,"ref":"","n":"Proclus suggests that inspired poets, looking to Orestes' murder of his mother, called him mountainous, savage and fruitless, having cut off his own source of generation, and passed the name to the Greeks.","q":"What then hinders but that poets, looking to the bold deed of Orestes towards his mother, may have called him Orestes, as ορειον, mountainous , and savage (αγριον), and without fruit (ακαρπον), as having cut off the principle of his proper generation; and that they should have delivered this name to the Greeks?"},{"s":1974,"ri":220,"ph":"the wildness, coarseness and ruggedness of his nature","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":174,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes Plato's method: he first states the thing signified, the savage and rustic, and only then the trace of it in the syllables, the mountainous.","q":"Thus in the name of Orestes he first says, that it signifies the savage and the rustic (το θηριωδες, και το αγριον), and afterwards he adds, το ορειον, the mountainous , which subsists in the syllables."},{"s":1975,"ri":224,"ph":"this man was admirable (agastos) for standing his ground (epimonē)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":175,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Plato, attending to the form of the name, derives Agamemnon from the admirable, while grammarians, attending to matter, derive it from the too much.","q":"That Plato, in his etymologies, despising the matter, but being especially attentive to the form of names, says that the name Agamemnon was composed from the admirable (παρα το αγαστον), and not from the too much (ου παρα το αγαν)."},{"s":1975,"ri":224,"ph":"his murder of Chrysippus and his cruel treatment of Thyestēs","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":177,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reads the passage morally: Orestes, Agamemnon and Atreus are all vehement men, but Orestes and Atreus are rightly blamed for sinning against mother and children.","q":"But since the first of these sinned against his mother, and the last towards his children, hence they are very properly blamed."},{"s":1975,"ri":224,"ph":"the length of his stay at Troy and his endurance","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":177,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains that Agamemnon is praised as admirable because he turned his vehemence against the barbarians, the natural enemies of the Greeks.","q":"But Agamemnon is called by him admirable and praise-worthy, because he exerted his vehemence on the naturally hostile race of the Barbarians."},{"s":1977,"ri":226,"ph":"he saw only what was close at hand and immediate or near (pelas)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":178,"ref":"","n":"Proclus draws the lesson that the Pelops story teaches us to despise appearances and look to the whole periods of souls, slack in human affairs but strenuous for virtue.","q":"That the particulars respecting Pelops teach us, first, to despise appearances, and to look to the whole periods of souls; and to be remiss with respect to human affairs, but strenuous with respect to virtue and things divine."},{"s":1977,"ri":226,"ph":"the far-reaching consequences for his entire family","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":178,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes the story to teach that children share the punishment for their ancestors' crimes, through kinship of soul, base seed and fortunes born of crime.","q":"And, in the second place, they teach us that children partake of the punishment belonging to the crimes of their ancestors."},{"s":1977,"ri":226,"ph":"if what we are told about Tantalus is true","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":179,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reads the Tantalus story as a soul raised by contemplation to the intelligible, the food of the gods, which then falls to earth and shares its new intellect with irrational nature.","q":"That the narration concerning Tantalus obscurely signifies a soul elevated through contemplation to the intelligible (for the intelligible is the nutriment of the gods); but falling from the intelligible place to earth, and communicating his intellectual life which remains recently perfected (νεοτελη) with the multitude of the irrational nature."},{"s":1978,"ri":228,"ph":"a stone was suspended (talanteia) over his head","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":179,"ref":"","n":"Proclus interprets the suspended stone as the earthy, stony, material burden hanging from a fallen soul, burying its intellect, which is its punishment in Hades.","q":"Such a soul too, being enfolded with the evils which are here, and associating with images instead of realities, is said to suffer punishment in Hades; having much of the terrestrial, stony, and material suspended from its nature, through which its intellectual part is buried."},{"s":1978,"ri":228,"ph":"his father, who is said to be Zeus","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":179,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains that Tantalus is called the son of Zeus because every newly perfected soul falls into generation from the court of Zeus.","q":"Hence he is said to be the son of Jupiter. For every recently-perfected soul falls from the court of Jupiter into generation;"},{"s":1978,"ri":228,"ph":"The circumstances of the legend seem to have provided this name for him","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":180,"ref":"","n":"Proclus distinguishes the allotment of a name to a life, which is the soul's work, from its general adoption, which comes from Fame, the messenger of Zeus.","q":"That the allotment of a certain name to a certain life is the work of the soul, but the general adoption of this name is from Fame."},{"s":1978,"ri":228,"ph":"his father, who is said to be Zeus","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":182,"ref":"","n":"Proclus distinguishes souls of whom Zeus truly is father, like Heracles, from fallen souls of whom he is only said to be father, as here.","q":"Such therefore as having been partakers of a certain energy above human nature, have again fallen into the sea of dissimilitude , and for honour among men have embraced error towards the gods,—of these Jupiter is said to be the father."},{"s":1979,"ri":228,"ph":"combining the two into one demonstrates the nature of the god","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":181,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Socrates rises from divine names, which are statues of the gods, to their powers and activities, leaving their essences to the flower of intellect.","q":"That Socrates from divine names, which are statues of the gods, recurs analytically to the powers and energies of the gods: for he leaves their essences, as being ineffable and unknown, to be alone surveyed by the flower of intellect."},{"s":1979,"ri":228,"ph":"although it is not easy to understand","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":194,"ref":"","n":"Proclus links this to the Timaeus: as the essence of the demiurge is hard to know, so his name, which reveals his power and activity, is not easy to understand.","q":"Timæus then says that it is difficult to know the essence of the demiurgus, and Socrates now says, that it is not easy to understand his name, which manifests his power and energy."},{"s":1979,"ri":228,"ph":"For some call him Zēn, others Dia","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":195,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains the twofold name: Dia signifies the cause through which, paternal goodness, and Zena signifies vivification, which the demiurge contains in unity.","q":"Very properly therefore is his name two-fold, of which δια manifests the cause through which , and this is paternal goodness; but ζηνα signifies vivification , the first causes of which in the universe the demiurgus unically comprehends."},{"s":1979,"ri":228,"ph":"the ruler and king of all","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":196,"ref":"","n":"Proclus distinguishes the two titles: Zeus is king as ranked with the fathers, and ruler as set directly above the triad of demiurges whose single cause he contains.","q":"And he is a king indeed, as being coordinated with the fathers; but a ruler , as being proximately established above the demiurgic triad, and comprehending the uniform cause of it."},{"s":1980,"ri":228,"ph":"the name, being one, is divided into two parts, Dia and Zēn","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":195,"ref":"","n":"Proclus makes Dia a symbol of the Saturnian, paternal series and Zen of maternal, life-giving Rhea, both contained in the demiurge, who establishes the duad in himself.","q":"The former, too, is a symbol of the Saturnian and paternal series; but the latter of the vivific and maternal Rhea."},{"s":1980,"ri":228,"ph":"all living creatures always have life (zēn)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":197,"ref":"","n":"Proclus distinguishes the world-soul, which gives life only to things moved by another, from the demiurge, who imparts every kind of life: divine, intellectual, psychic and bodily.","q":"But the demiurgus simply imparts to all things life divine, intellectual, psychical, and that which is divisible about bodies."},{"s":1980,"ri":228,"ph":"he is the son of Cronus","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":197,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains Zeus's sonship as procession, not separation: the demiurgic intellect proceeds from a higher, more unified intellect, Cronus, and unfolds what is hidden in him.","q":"For Jupiter being the demiurgic intellect, proceeds from another intellect, superior and more uniform, which increases indeed its proper intellections, but converts the multitude of them to union;"},{"s":1980,"ri":228,"ph":"might seem disrespectful on first hearing","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":198,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains why the name of Cronus sounds insolent: to a hasty hearer it suggests koros, satiety, and the poets say that satiety gives birth to insolence.","q":"We reply, that according to the poets satiety (κορος) is the cause of insolence ; for they thus denominate immoderation and repletion; and they say that Satiety brought forth Insolence (υβριν φασιν τικτει κορος)."},{"s":1980,"ri":228,"ph":"To suggest that he is the son of Cronus might seem disrespectful","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":200,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Plato mentions the apparent insolence because theologians call Cronus's violent succession insolent, so the name fits the god and mirrors the myth.","q":"Plato, therefore, seeing this succession, which in Saturn is called by theologists insolent (υβριστικη), thought it worth while to mention the appearance of insolence in the name; that from this he might evince the name is adapted to the god,"},{"s":1980,"ri":228,"ph":"huge","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":201,"ref":"","n":"Proclus warns that greatness, said of the gods, means power of cause and intellectual eminence, not spatial magnitude.","q":"That the great , when ascribed to the gods, must not be considered as belonging to interval, but as subsisting intellectually, and according to the power of cause, but not according to partible transcedency."},{"s":1980,"ri":228,"ph":"Zeus is the offspring of some huge intellect","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":201,"ref":"","n":"Proclus asks why Cronus is called dianoia, discursive thought, and answers that Plato looks to the multitude of intellectual conceptions and the unfolding of forms within him.","q":"But why does Plato now call Saturn διανοια, the dianoëtic part of the soul? May we not say, that it is because he looks to the multitude of intellectual conceptions in him, the orders of intelligibles, and the evolution of forms which he contains;"},{"s":1980,"ri":228,"ph":"some huge intellect","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":201,"ref":"","n":"Proclus finds both ancient etymologies of the Titans here: great signifies power extending to all things, and some (a certain) signifies power reaching the most particular natures.","q":"For the term great is a symbol of power pervading to all things; but the term a certain , of power proceeding to the most partial natures."},{"s":1980,"ri":228,"ph":"koros (representing Cronus) does not indicate a child","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":202,"ref":"","n":"Proclus counts three analyses of the name: satiety is rejected as insolent, and the second, suggesting the immature and childish, is likewise rejected.","q":"The second also, which exhibits the imperfect and the puerile, is in like manner rejected."},{"s":1980,"ri":228,"ph":"the pure (kathoros) and unblemished nature of reason (nous)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":202,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says the third analysis is approved, which praises Cronus as full of purity, leader of undefiled intelligence and an undeviating life.","q":"But the third, which celebrates this god as full of purity, and as the leader of undefiled intelligence, and an undeviating life, is approved."},{"s":1980,"ri":228,"ph":"the pure (kathoros)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":202,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes purity to signify Cronus's transcendence: freedom from contact with matter, impartibility, and exemption from relation to lower things.","q":"Purity , therefore, indicates this impartible and imparticipable transcendency of Saturn. For the not coming into contact with matter, the impartible, and an exemption from habitude, are signified by purity."},{"s":1980,"ri":228,"ph":"koros (representing Cronus)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":207,"ref":"","n":"Proclus glosses the name as koronous, pure intellect: immaterial, undivided, and established in the paternal silence.","q":"Hence he is κορονους, as an immaterial and pure intellect, and as establishing himself in the paternal silence."},{"s":1981,"ri":229,"ph":"the upward vision is rightly called 'ourania'","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":210,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Plato here derives Heaven's energy from its name, its looking towards higher and simpler things nearer the One, showing Heaven as both intelligible and intellectual.","q":"Here therefore, investigating the truth of things from names, he declares its energy with respect to things more elevated and simple, and which are arranged nearer to the one ."},{"s":1981,"ri":229,"ph":"looking upwards","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":210,"ref":"","n":"Proclus asks what the things above are that Heaven beholds, and identifies them with the supercelestial place of the Phaedrus and the whole intelligible extent.","q":"What then are the things on high which it beholds? Is it not evident that they are the supercelestial place, an essence without colour, without figure, and without the touch, and all the intelligible extent?"},{"s":1981,"ri":229,"ph":"those who study the heavens","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":211,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes the term for those who discourse on things on high in an anagogic sense: those who live intellectually, do not gravitate to earth, and rise to a contemplative life.","q":"With respect to the term μετεωρολογοι, i. e. those who discourse on sub- lime affairs , we must now consider it in a manner adapted to those who choose an anagogic life, who live intellectually, and who do not gravitate to earth, but sublimely tend to a theoretic life."},{"s":1981,"ri":229,"ph":"this results in pure reason and Uranus has the correct name","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":211,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes that after praising Cronus for intelligence turned towards himself, Plato praises Heaven for a more perfect activity, union with higher natures.","q":"Plato therefore having celebrated Saturn for his intelligence, which is without habitude to mundane natures, and for his life which is converted to his own exalted place of survey, now celebrates Heaven for another more perfect energy: for to be conjoined to more elevated natures, is a greater good than to be converted to oneself."},{"s":1981,"ri":229,"ph":"the upward vision","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":212,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says that by the single act of looking upwards Socrates reveals all Heaven's powers at once, its converting, guarding and connecting of what comes after it.","q":"but now Socrates at once manifests all the powers of Heaven, through convertive energy. For this is to behold things on high, to be converted to them, and through this to be connected and defended."},{"s":1981,"ri":229,"ph":"he, the story goes, is the son of Uranus","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":213,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains the myth of the castrations: Cronus, as a dividing Titan, cuts his kingdom off from Heaven and, as pure intellect, is cut off from Zeus's material fabrication.","q":"Saturn therefore, as a dividing god, separates his kingdom from that of Heaven; but as a pure intellect, he is exempt from a fabricative energy proceeding into matter."},{"s":1981,"ri":229,"ph":"if I could have remembered the genealogy of Hesiod","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":214,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Socrates blames his memory not because he disbelieves the myths of causes older than Heaven or thinks them unworthy of mention.","q":"He accuses therefore his memory, not as disbelieving in the fables, which assert, that there are certain more antient causes beyond Heaven, nor as not thinking it worth while to mention them."},{"s":1982,"ri":229,"ph":"I could have continued explaining that they too had correct names","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":214,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains Socrates' restraint: the first beings cannot be known through memory, imagination, opinion or discursive thought, and so cannot properly be named.","q":"But he says this, because the first of beings cannot become known by the exercise of memory, and through phantasy, or opinion, or the dianoëtic part."},{"s":1982,"ri":229,"ph":"where he describes even earlier ancestors of these gods","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":218,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Plato mentions Hesiod here because Hesiod passes over in silence the natures prior to Heaven as ineffable.","q":"And on this account Plato now thinks Hesiod deserves to be mentioned, for passing by the natures prior to Heaven, as being ineffable."},{"s":1983,"ri":231,"ph":"I blame what has happened to me on Euthyphro of Prospaltia","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":219,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Socrates names Euthyphro not as the source of this knowledge but as one whose fantastic talk about the gods provokes a scientific search for truth.","q":"Hence, he ascribes the cause of this descent in speculation to Euthyphro; not considering him as the leader of this knowledge, but as one who, through the phantastically prodigious nature of his discourse, excites to the scientific investigation of truth."},{"s":1984,"ri":231,"ph":"let's make use of this wisdom and continue our investigation into the remaining names","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":219,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains that Socrates comes down from science to meet the many, whose notions of the gods are as depraved as Euthyphro's, and lifts them to opinion grounded in the name-giver.","q":"Socrates, perceiving that the conceptions of the multitude about the gods were equally depraved with those of Euthyphro, descends from a scientific energy to inferior concerns, but at the same time elevates those who are detained by phantasy to a middle habit of apprehension concerning the gods."},{"s":1984,"ri":231,"ph":"either a priest or a sophist, who is adept in such purifications","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":226,"ref":"","n":"Proclus compares the two purifiers: priests remove what blocks our perception of higher things, as angels do, while sophists cut away false opinion by arguing both sides, as daemons do.","q":"so indeed the ministers of sacred rites, angelically remove from us every thing which impedes our perception of more excellent nature; but sophists, through exercising us dæmoniacally by arguments on both sides, cut off the injury which we sustain from false opinion;"},{"s":1985,"ri":233,"ph":"the names in use for heroes and humans","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":220,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says the daemons and heroes mentioned here are such by condition, not by nature: souls like Heracles who descend into generation and do not always follow the gods.","q":"Those dæmons however that are now mentioned, together with the demigods heroes, are not dæmons and heroes by nature, for they do not always follow the gods; but they are only so from habitude,"},{"s":1986,"ri":233,"ph":"those that are correctly given when dealing with whatever is eternal and natural","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":228,"ref":"","n":"Proclus divides names into those of eternal and those of perishable things, and says that names of eternal things are devised partly by men and partly by more divine causes.","q":"That of names some belong to perpetual, and others to corruptible things. And of those which belong to things perpetual, some are devised by men, but others by more divine causes."},{"s":1986,"ri":233,"ph":"some of them were given by a power more divine than any human power","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":228,"ref":"","n":"Proclus specifies that, of names coming from causes more divine than man, some are established by the gods themselves and others by daemons.","q":"And of those which are the production of more divine causes than men, some are established by the gods themselves, but others by dæmons."},{"s":1986,"ri":233,"ph":"given in a spirit of prayer","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":228,"ref":"","n":"Proclus classes names of perishable things given without art: some depend on an unknown divine cause, like Orestes, and others are given from hope, from memory, or from neither.","q":"And of those without a cause, some subsist according to hope, others according to memory, and others according to neither of these."},{"s":1987,"ri":237,"ph":"sun, moon, stars and sky","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":230,"ref":"","n":"Proclus contrasts these first Greeks with the strange inhabitants of Greece in his own day, who do not regard the sun and moon as gods or worship the heavenly bodies.","q":"That it is the peculiarity of the strange inhabitants of Greece of the present time, neither to consider the sun nor moon as gods, nor to worship the other celestials, though they are our saviours and guides,"},{"s":1987,"ri":237,"ph":"Later, recognising the other gods, they called them all by this same name","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":231,"ref":"","n":"Proclus holds that the name god rightly extends from the visible gods to supercelestial, intellectual and intelligible causes, which also have a swift, timeless motion.","q":"That the name GOD is rationally ascribed not only to the apparent, but also to the supercelestial, intellectual, and intelligible causes."},{"s":1987,"ri":237,"ph":"they named them 'gods' (theous)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":233,"ref":"","n":"Proclus offers a second etymology of theos, from tithenai, to place, meaning to make, so that the name shows the gods as productive causes of all things.","q":"This name may also be interpreted after another manner; since it manifests the producing and fabricative causes of all things: for θειναι and θησω are assumed for το ποιειν."},{"s":1989,"ri":245,"ph":"the first race of humans to be born was a golden race","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":235,"ref":"","n":"Proclus makes the golden race correspond to the intellectual genus of higher beings, since gold is referred to the first, empyrean and intellectual world.","q":"That the hyparxis of the more excellent genera being triple, viz. intellectual, logical, phantastic, the golden age is analogous to the intellectual genus."},{"s":1990,"ri":252,"ph":"he refers to ourselves as an iron race","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":235,"ref":"","n":"Proclus likens the fifth, human age to laboured black iron because of the material and dark condition of its life and its erring actions.","q":"And the fifth and very passive human age, is that which is assimilated to much-laboured and black iron, through the material and dark condition of its life."},{"s":1991,"ri":258,"ph":"he called them daimōnes","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":234,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Socrates here calls all three higher genera daemons, and that his etymology from wisdom transcends and may displease the material daemons, who delight in division.","q":"These three genera, therefore, which are more excellent than us, Socrates now calls dæmons. But if he displeases the material tribe of dæmons by this etymology, you must not wonder."},{"s":1991,"ri":258,"ph":"anyone who is good is daimōnic, whether they be living or deceased","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":236,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Plato reasons by analogy from daemons by condition, such as good men, up to daemons above our essence, while passing over material daemons.","q":"That Plato now speculates about dæmons and heroes, not those which subsist according to habitude, but those which are beyond our essence. He recurs however, through analogy, from those which subsist according to habitude, to those of a more elevated order."},{"s":1991,"ri":258,"ph":"in our ancient language this very name is found","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":237,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains the ancient form: daemons were once called damons, the letter alpha then being used in place of the diphthong ai.","q":"That in the antient tongue dæmons were called damons , is evident from a being then used instead of αι."},{"s":1992,"ri":260,"ph":"it indicates their birth from love (erōs)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":224,"ref":"","n":"Proclus accepts that heroes are rightly named from Love, since Love is a great daemon and heroes are born through the cooperation of daemons.","q":"That heroes are very properly denominated from Love, since Love is a great dæmon: and from the cooperation of dæmons, heroes are produced."},{"s":1992,"ri":262,"ph":"Don't you know that heroes are demigods?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":223,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains that heroes are called demigods because only one of their two soul-circles, sameness or difference, is illuminated by the gods.","q":"On this account heroes are called demigods (ημιθεοι), as having only one of their circles illuminated by the gods."},{"s":1993,"ri":264,"ph":"born from the love (erōs) of a god or a goddess for a mortal","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":221,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains that gods do not mingle with mortals; the lowest powers of a god's series assist human generation, and fables give them the god's name.","q":"Fables therefore, through the similitude of appellation, conceal the things themselves. For spirits of this kind are similarly denominated with the gods, the leading causes of their series. Hence they say, either that gods have connexion with women, or men with goddesses."},{"s":1993,"ri":264,"ph":"the love (erōs) of a god","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":223,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reads a divine father as meaning that the hero's circle of sameness, the intellectual life, is unfettered, with a defect in the life of opinion.","q":"Such of these therefore as have the circle of sameness unfettered, as are excited to an intellectual life, and are moved about it according to a deific energy,—these are said to have a god for their father, and a mortal for their mother, through a defect with respect to the doxastic form of life."},{"s":1993,"ri":264,"ph":"a goddess for a mortal","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":223,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reads a divine mother as meaning that the hero acts rightly and inspiredly in practical life through the circle of difference, as Achilles does.","q":"But such, on the contrary, as energize without impediment according to the circle of difference, who act with becoming rectitude in practical affairs, and at the same time enthusiastically , or, in other words, under the inspiring influence of divinity,—these are said to have a mortal for their father, and a goddess for their mother."},{"s":1993,"ri":264,"ph":"‘hero’ is a slight alteration of the name of ‘love’ (erōs)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":234,"ref":"","n":"Proclus adds that heroes are named as those able to raise and lift souls up to the gods, leading them upwards through love.","q":"Hence they are allotted this appellation as being able to raise and extend souls to the gods (ως αιρειν και ανατεινειν τας ψυχας επι θεους δυναμενα)."},{"s":1995,"ri":268,"ph":"such an ingenious notion","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":225,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes that in Plato the word kompson (ingenious, clever) means both elegant and fitting, and also persuasive and deceptive.","q":"That according to Plato το κομψον signifies both the elegant and the ap- propriate (τοτε κομψον και οικειον); and again it signifies the persuasive and the deceitful (το πιθανον και απατηλον):"},{"s":1995,"ri":268,"ph":"we sometimes change the accents","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":238,"ref":"","n":"Proclus distinguishes the parts of a name: syllables and letters are like its essence, while acute and grave accents are like its powers.","q":"That syllables and letters appear to have the relation of essence in names, but acute and grave accents are certain powers of names."},{"s":2003,"ri":294,"ph":"nor about the precise names they call themselves, which are of course true names","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":241,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains that each god, knowing itself and the other divine kinds, gives rise to divine names that remain unknown and ineffable to us.","q":"That each of the gods, so far as he knows himself, and all the other divine genera, and participates of all things, and is bounded according to his proper hyparxis, so far he gives subsistence to divine names which are to us unknown and ineffable;"},{"s":2003,"ri":294,"ph":"the names they love to be called by in our conventional prayers","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":243,"ref":"","n":"Proclus holds that divine names vary with the rank of the namer: gods, angels, daemons and souls each name the gods in a way proportioned to their essence and knowledge.","q":"That according to the analogous of essences and knowledges in the gods, angels, dæmons, and souls, the mutation also of divine names subsists."},{"s":2003,"ri":294,"ph":"nor about the precise names they call themselves, which are of course true names","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":229,"ref":"","n":"Proclus, quoted by Taylor, places the first and truly divine names in the gods themselves, with the names of the second order, their intellectual resemblances, belonging to daemons.","q":"The first, most principal, and truly divine names must be considered as established in the gods themselves. But those of the second order, and which are the resemblances of these, subsisting in an intellectual manner, must be said to be of a dæmoniacal condition."},{"s":2004,"ri":294,"ph":"human beings and the opinions they held when they gave the gods those names","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":241,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says that since the soul holds conceptions of the gods only as images and not coordinately with intellect, it can grasp the essence and naming of divinity only after the manner of an image.","q":"For it can only receive conceptions about the essence and the nomination of divinity, after the manner of an image (εικονικως)."},{"s":2006,"ri":296,"ph":"begin in the customary manner with Hestia","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":245,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains the custom from prayer: worshippers are called on to hymn Hestia first, as the oldest of the gods.","q":"For in prayers they are called upon to celebrate Vesta before the other gods."},{"s":2007,"ri":302,"ph":"it is reasonable to call the being of things 'Hestia'","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":246,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says those who derive Hestia from essence are looking to the goddess's own proper existence.","q":"Hence, some say that Vesta is denominated from essence (απο της εστιας), looking to her proper hyparxis."},{"s":2007,"ri":302,"ph":"whatever partakes of being 'is' (estin)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":247,"ref":"","n":"Proclus specifies that Hestia signifies not essence simply but essence abiding firmly in itself, which is why she comes forth after Cronus.","q":"That Vesta does not manifest essence, but the abiding and firm establishment of essence in itself; and hence this goddess proceeds into light after the mighty Saturn."},{"s":2008,"ri":302,"ph":"their cause and ruler is that which pushes (ōthoun)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":246,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains the second etymology, Hestia as cause of impulsion, as looking to the life-giving, moving power she shares with Hera, since divine natures participate in one another.","q":"But others looking to her vivific and motive power, which she derives from Juno, say that she is thus denominated, ως ωσεως ουσαν αιτιαν, as being the cause of impulsion."},{"s":2010,"ri":310,"ph":"he compares things that are to the flow of a river","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":249,"ref":"","n":"Proclus warns that Socrates does not adopt Heraclitus' material flux, which is false and unworthy of Plato, so the talk of flowing must be taken with caution.","q":"That we ought to receive with caution what is now said concerning effluxions and motions. For Socrates does not descend to the material flowing of Heraclitus; for this is false, and unworthy the dianoëtic conceptions of Plato."},{"s":2011,"ri":312,"ph":"he gave the names of streams to both of them","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":249,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Socrates likens the fontal, Saturnian deities to streams half in jest and half seriously, because good always flows down from above like streams.","q":"Socrates very properly assimilates fontal and Saturnian deities to streams; in so doing jesting, and at the same time acting seriously, because good is always derived, as it were, in streams from on high, to things below."},{"s":2011,"ri":312,"ph":"the names Rhea and Cronus","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":253,"ref":"","n":"Proclus derives Rhea from her causing a perpetual flow of good and from her being the cause of divine ease, since the gods are said to live at ease.","q":"Rhea therefore is so called from causing a perpetual influx of good, and through being the cause of divine facility , since the life of the gods is attended with ease (θεοι ρεια ζωοντες)."},{"s":2011,"ri":312,"ph":"Ocean the origin of the gods, and mother Tethys","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":254,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Ocean and Tethys are fontal gods in their first rank, which is why Socrates calls them fathers of streams.","q":"Both Ocean and Tethys therefore are fontal gods, according to their first subsistence. Hence Socrates now calls them the fathers of streams."},{"s":2012,"ri":313,"ph":"First to wed was fair flowing Ocean","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":256,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains divine marriage as theological language for gods arranged together and cooperating in their productions, as Ocean with Tethys.","q":"For an according coarrangement of the gods, and a connascent cooperation in their productions, is called by theologists mar- riage."},{"s":2012,"ri":314,"ph":"Who took to wife his sister, Tethys, his mother's child","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":263,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Plato, through the two pairs Cronus-Rhea and Ocean-Tethys, embraces the whole Titanic order, both in its procession and in its abiding with Heaven.","q":"What is now said, therefore, by Plato, comprehends all the Titannic order through these two conjunctions; this order abiding and at the same time proceeding."},{"s":2012,"ri":316,"ph":"a disguised name for a spring","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":254,"ref":"","n":"Proclus assigns the powers: Ocean gives the higher natures vigour of activity, while Tethys gives the leaping forth and filtering of what passes to lower natures.","q":"And vigour of energy indeed is present with more antient natures, through Ocean; but the leaping forth and percolating through Tethys."},{"s":2012,"ri":316,"ph":"that which sifts (diattomenon) and filters (ēthoumenon) represents a spring","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":257,"ref":"","n":"Proclus gives the etymology: Tethys is named from leaping forth and straining, as if Diatethys, with the first two syllables removed.","q":"That Tethys is denominated from leaping forth and straining or cleans- ing , being as it were Diatethys , and by taking away the first two syallables, Tethys ."},{"s":2013,"ri":318,"ph":"Zeus, we have already explained","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":264,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains why Zeus is not discussed with his brothers: the head of the demiurgic triad shares the name of the whole demiurgic intellect already treated.","q":"That of the demiurgic triad which divides the whole world, and distributes the indivisible, one, and whole fabrication of the first Jupiter, the summit, and which has the relation of father, is Jupiter, who through union with the whole demiurgic intellect, having the same appellation with it, is for this reason not mentioned here by Plato."},{"s":2014,"ri":322,"ph":"the nature of the sea hindered him as he proceeded","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":265,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains the first analysis of Poseidon's name from his realm and from souls entering generation, whose circle of sameness is fettered, the sea standing for generation.","q":"And the first analyzation of his name is from the allotment over which he presides, and from souls coming into generation, in whom the circle of sameness is fettered; since the sea is analogous to generation."},{"s":2015,"ri":322,"ph":"because of his shaking activity","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":264,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes Poseidon, the middle of the demiurgic triad, as the cause of motion to all things, hence called earth-shaker.","q":"But the middle is said to be the cause of motion to all things. Hence also, he is called earth-shaker , as being the origin of motion."},{"s":2015,"ri":322,"ph":"because the god knows (eidota) a great deal (polla)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":267,"ref":"","n":"Proclus derives the second analysis, knowing many things, from Poseidon's communion with the first Zeus, who is his nearest intelligible, citing Homer that Zeus was born first and knew more.","q":"For a Jupiter of this kind, is the proximate intelligible of Neptune."},{"s":2015,"ri":322,"ph":"he was named 'the Shaker' (ho seiōn)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":267,"ref":"","n":"Proclus derives the third analysis from Poseidon's outward activity: he moves nature, gives life to the lowest things, and stirs the earth to generation.","q":"But the third analysis of his name, is from his energy in externals. For he is motive of nature, and vivific of things last."},{"s":2015,"ri":322,"ph":"Pluto is named as the giver of wealth (ploutos)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":269,"ref":"","n":"Proclus rejects reading Pluto as earthly wealth of crops and metals, and Hades as the invisible, dark and dreadful.","q":"That some badly analyze the name of Pluto into wealth from the earth, through fruits and metals; but Hades into the invisible, dark, and dreadful."},{"s":2015,"ri":322,"ph":"most people assume that his name refers to the unseen (aides)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":269,"ref":"","n":"Proclus denies that Hades is evil; death is not evil, and the unseen is better than the visible, as everything intelligible is.","q":"For Hades is not, as some improperly explain it, evil: for neither is death evil; though Hades to some appears to be attended with peturbations (εμπαθως); but it is invisible, and better than the apparent; such as is every thing intelligible."},{"s":2015,"ri":322,"ph":"the god knows (eidota) a great deal (polla)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":278,"ref":"","n":"Proclus contrasts the epithets: Poseidon, compared with Zeus, knows many things, while Hades, compared with the souls he teaches, knows all things, though Poseidon is the more universal.","q":"That Neptune when compared with Jupiter is said to know many things; but Hades compared with souls to whom he imparts knowledge is said to know all things; though Neptune is more total than Hades."},{"s":2016,"ri":324,"ph":"are unjustifiably afraid of him","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":270,"ref":"","n":"Proclus attributes the fear of death to lovers of the body, who wrongly ascribe the body's passions to themselves, though life in Hades is more natural than embodied life.","q":"That men who are lovers of body, badly refer to themselves the passions of the animated nature, and on this account consider death to be dreadful, as being the cause of corruption."},{"s":2017,"ri":332,"ph":"there are many desires, are there not?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":271,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Plato considers desire in each part of the soul: the spirited part desires honour or victory, the rational part virtue.","q":"That Plato contemplates desire, according to each part of the soul. For the irascible part aspires after honour or victory, and the rational after virtue."},{"s":2018,"ri":338,"ph":"the god is a perfect sophist","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":269,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains why Hades is called a sophist: he purifies souls after death and frees them from generation.","q":"For this god is a sophist, who purifying souls after death, frees them from generation."},{"s":2018,"ri":338,"ph":"a perfect sophist","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":274,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes that Plato can give the name sophist an honourable sense, applying it to whoever can turn others towards himself, such as Zeus, Hades and Love.","q":"That Plato knew how to attribute the name sophist to a venerable thing: for he thus denominates him who is able to convert other things to himself, such as Jupiter, Hades, and Love."},{"s":2018,"ri":338,"ph":"not even the Sirens themselves","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":273,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Plato knew three kinds of Sirens, celestial, generative and cathartic; those in Hades draw gods, daemons and souls round Pluto.","q":"So that Plato knew that in the kingdom of Hades there are gods, dæmons, and souls, who dance as it were round Pluto, allured by the Sirens that dwell there."},{"s":2019,"ri":339,"ph":"he does associate with us once the soul is purified","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":275,"ref":"","n":"Proclus adds that not every freed soul is fit to be with Pluto; more bodily souls are first purged of vice, painfully, by cathartic daemons or angels.","q":"That not all souls, after being liberated from the body, are thought worthy to associate with Pluto, but such only as are of superior worth: for those that are more corporeal are liberated from vice, by certain cathartic dæmons or angels, laboriously, and accompanied with pain."},{"s":2019,"ri":339,"ph":"his father, Cronus","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":276,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains Zeus binding Cronus as Zeus turning towards and depending on him, establishing Cronus within himself, so that each is bound in the other.","q":"Jupiter therefore binds Saturn in himself firmly and stably; and Jupiter is in a similar manner bound in Saturn."},{"s":2019,"ri":339,"ph":"binding them with his famous shackles","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":277,"ref":"","n":"Proclus distinguishes two ascents, through the bonds of Pluto and of Cronus, and says neither can hold souls still stunned by the body, though Cronus's bonds are stronger.","q":"Not only the bonds of Hades therefore are incapable of holding and elevating to the dominion of Jupiter, souls that are astonished about body, but neither can the bonds of Saturn effect this, though these as being of the father are evidently stronger."},{"s":2021,"ri":341,"ph":"he knows (eidenai) all that is good","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":269,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Socrates brings both names to one meaning: Pluto as intellect rich in wisdom, Hades as intellect that knows all things.","q":"These Socrates now reprobates, bringing the two names to the same signification; referring the name of Pluto, as intellect, to the wealth of prudence, but that of Hades to an intellect knowing all things."},{"s":2021,"ri":342,"ph":"Dēmēter, Hēra, Apollo, Athena","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":292,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes that Socrates treats the three life-giving goddesses in order: Demeter, Hera and Persephone, mother, sister and daughter of the demiurge.","q":"But Socrates now delivers these three vivific monads in a consequent order; viz. Ceres, Juno, Proserpine; calling the first the mother, the second the sister, and the third the daughter of the demiurgus."},{"s":2021,"ri":343,"ph":"she gives (didousa) the gift of food","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":279,"ref":"","n":"Proclus extends Demeter's gift of food beyond bodily nourishment: she nourishes first the gods, then lower natures, and only last bodies.","q":"so likewise Ceres must be analyzed not only into corporeal nutriment; but, beginning from the gods themselves, it is requisite to conceive her to be the supplier of aliment, first to the gods themselves, afterwards to the natures posterior to the gods; and, in the last place, that the series of this beneficent energy extends as far as to corporeal nutriment."},{"s":2021,"ri":343,"ph":"like a mother (mētēr)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":284,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains the two titles: Demeter is mother because she contains the cause of Hera, and giver of food because she contains Hestia.","q":"Hence, Ceres is with great propriety called by Plato, mother , and at the same time the supplier of aliment : for, so far as she comprehends in herself the cause of Juno, she is a mother; but as containing Vesta in her essence, she is the supplier of aliment."},{"s":2021,"ri":343,"ph":"like a mother (mētēr), she gives (didousa) the gift of food","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":286,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says earthly mothers, giving birth and then milk, imitate Demeter's twofold eternal generation of life and of what perfects life.","q":"Hence mothers, who are connected with the circulations of time, bring forth their offspring in imitation of this two-fold and eternal generation of Ceres. For, at the same time that they send forth their young into the light, they extend to them milk naturally produced as their food."},{"s":2021,"ri":343,"ph":"Zeus is said to have married her for love","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":287,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says the demiurgic intellect is joined to the mother above it by conversion, to Persephone below it by providence, and to Hera, its coordinate, by love.","q":"For it is present with the mother prior to itself, convertively (επιστρεπτικως); with Proserpine posterior to itself, providen- tially (προνοητικως); and with Juno coordinate to itself with an amatory energy (ερασμιως)."},{"s":2021,"ri":343,"ph":"called the air (aera) Hēra","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":290,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains that Hera's series reaches the lowest things and her sublunary allotment is the air, a symbol of soul.","q":"That the series of our sovereign mistress Juno beginning from on high pervades to the last of things; and her allotment in the sublunary region is the air."},{"s":2021,"ri":343,"ph":"You would recognise this if you were to repeat the name Hera over and over","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":292,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reads the joining of the name's end to its beginning as rational souls turning back to Hera from whom they proceed, voice being struck air.","q":"The assertion likewise that the end of this name will be conjoined with the beginning, if any one frequently repeats the name of the goddess, evinces the conversion of rational souls to her which proceed from her; and that voice is struck air."},{"s":2021,"ri":343,"ph":"this name is feared by many, and so too is the name Apollo","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":294,"ref":"","n":"Proclus draws the moral that a pious person must hold to the correctness of divine names, so as not to incur Socrates' rebuke to those who err about Persephone and Apollo.","q":"That a lover of piety to the gods ought earnestly to embrace the rectitude of divine names, lest, like those who err concerning Proserpine and Apollo, being ignorant of the analysis of names, he should be subject to the same reproof from Socrates."},{"s":2021,"ri":343,"ph":"Hēra is the lovely one (eratē) because Zeus is said to have married her for love","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-1","p":152,"ref":"","n":"Proclus cites this etymology as Socrates' own testimony that Homer's account of Zeus' union with Hera is theological, deriving Hera's name from love.","q":"which is also testified by Socrates in the Cratylus, who derives the etymology of Juno from nothing else than love , as being, says he, lovely to Jupiter."},{"s":2022,"ri":343,"ph":"bringer of slaughter, pherein phonon","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":295,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reinterprets the fearful name: Persephone separates forms from one another, or separates souls wholly from bodies, the most fortunate kind of slaughter and death.","q":"That Proserpine is denominated either through judging of forms and separating them from each other, thus obscurely signifying the ablation of slaughter (δια το κρινειν τα ειδη και Χωριζειν αλληλων, ως του φονου την αναιρεσιν αινιττομενον), or through separating souls perfectly from bodies, through a conversion to things on high, which is the most fortunat e slaughter and death, to such as are worthy of it"},{"s":2023,"ri":343,"ph":"her contact (epaphē) with that which is moving (pheromenon)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":295,"ref":"","n":"Proclus applies the name Pherephatta to Persephone in her contact with generation, but in its sense of wisdom and counsel to Athena.","q":"But the name Φερεφαττα, Pherephatta, according to a contact with generation, is adapted to Proserpine; but according to wisdom and counsel, to Minerva."},{"s":2023,"ri":343,"ph":"That's why Hades, who is wise, associates with her","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":295,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains that it is Persephone, the middle of the goddess's triad, who was carried off by Pluto, while her extremes remain virgin.","q":"On this account also she is called Proserpine, and not by the names of the extremes; since that which was ravished by Pluto is this middle;"},{"s":2025,"ri":347,"ph":"the four powers of the god","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":301,"ref":"","n":"Proclus notes that theologians transmit a great multitude of Apolline properties, but Socrates mentions only four of them.","q":"Theologists indeed deliver to us a great multitude of Apolloniacal idioms; but Socrates now only mentions four of them."},{"s":2025,"ri":349,"ph":"the purifications and purgations, both in medicine and in prophecy","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":308,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says purification in both medicine and prophecy shows that Apollo's cathartic power contains both powers, illuminating with light and purging with healing energies.","q":"That purification being seen not only in the medicinal, but also in the prophetic art, evinces, that the cathartic power of Apollo comprehends the two powers:"},{"s":2025,"ri":349,"ph":"the sprinklings too","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":308,"ref":"","n":"Proclus connects the sprinklings with theurgy: the priest of Apollo begins from purifications and sprinklings with sea water, as the Oracle prescribes.","q":"Hence the theurgist, who is the leader of the mysteries of this god, begins from purifications and sprinklings:"},{"s":2026,"ri":353,"ph":"what's true and what's simple (aplous), which are indeed the same","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":310,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Apollo's presiding over simplicity and truth makes him analogous to the Good of the Republic, the simple manifesting the One.","q":"But the assertion that the god presides over simplicity according to knowledge, and unfolds truth into light, presents him to our view as analogous to the good, which Socrates celebrates in the Republic;"},{"s":2027,"ri":353,"ph":"a master archer who is ever-darting (aei-bolōn)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":310,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes the ever-prevailing archery as Apollo's dominion over the world, his arrows signifying the rays he pours from the supercelestial order.","q":"But the perpetually prevailing might of the god in the jaculation of arrows, evinces his dominion, which vanquishes every thing in the world. For on high, from the supercelestial order, he scatters the rivers of Jupiter, and pours his rays on the whole world: for his arrows obscurely signify his rays."},{"s":2027,"ri":353,"ph":"movement together around the sky, around the poles","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":310,"ref":"","n":"Proclus sees the whole celestial order and its motion as the harmonious work of Apollo, to which souls are perfected by becoming harmonically like the universe.","q":"The whole of this celestial order too, and motion, exhibit the harmonious work of the god;"},{"s":2028,"ri":353,"ph":"although he was called 'Omopolōn', we called him 'Apollo'","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":301,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says the first and most natural sense of Apollo's name is the cause of union, the power that gathers multitude into one.","q":"It is necessary, however, to know thus much concerning Apollo, that, according to the first and most natural conception, his name signifies the cause of union, and that power which collects multitude into one;"},{"s":2028,"ri":353,"ph":"this god Apollo supervises the harmony by moving them all together","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":310,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes Apollo's presiding over music to mean that he causes all harmony, visible and invisible, and with Mnemosyne and Zeus begets the Muses.","q":"Again, the assertion that he presides over music, represents to us that this god is the cause of all harmony, both unapparent and apparent, through his ruling supermundane powers, according to which he generates, together with Mnemosyne and Jupiter, the Muses."},{"s":2029,"ri":353,"ph":"the name encompasses all the powers of the god","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":301,"ref":"","n":"Proclus asks why Socrates goes from the healing power through prophecy and archery to harmony, and answers that each power predominates in a different order of being.","q":"Why then does Socrates use an order of this kind? For, beginning from the medicinal power of the god, and proceeding through his prophetic and arrow-darting powers, he ends in his harmonic power."},{"s":2030,"ri":354,"ph":"The Muses, and the realm of music in general","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":311,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains why the Muses follow Apollo: he is Musagetes, the monad of cosmic harmony, while the choir of the Muses is the monad of the number nine.","q":"From discoursing about king Apollo, Plato proceeds to the Muses, and the name of music: for Apollo is celebrated as Musagetes;"},{"s":2030,"ri":354,"ph":"from philosophic enquiry","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":312,"ref":"","n":"Proclus connects the etymology with Plato calling philosophy the greatest music, since it moves the soul's powers in harmony with real beings and the heavenly motions.","q":"That such being the etymology of the name of the Muses, since Plato calls philosophy the greatest music, as causing our psychical powers to be moved harmoniously, in symphony with real beings, and in conformity to the orderly motions of the celestial orbs;"},{"s":2030,"ri":354,"ph":"derive their name from the word ‘mōsthai’ (to seek after)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":312,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains why the Muses are named from seeking: inquiry stands to discovery as matter to end, multitude to the One, and variety to simplicity.","q":"For investigations have the relation of matter, with reference to the end from invention; just as multitude with respect to the one, and variety with respect to simplicity."},{"s":2030,"ri":354,"ph":"her willingness (ethelēmona) to grant whatever anyone asks for","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":313,"ref":"","n":"Proclus derives Leto from willing or from smooth, both showing her powers: she gives souls a voluntary life and smoothness of manners.","q":"Whether, therefore, she is called Leto, from a voluntary life; for λω signifies το βουλομαι, I am willing ; or from το λειον, the smooth ; her name will perfectly evince, through both these, the powers which she possesses."},{"s":2030,"ri":354,"ph":"her character is not harsh, but gentle and smooth (leion)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":313,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains the smoothness: material roughness makes the soul's activities compelled, while ascent to the gods makes life smooth, gentle and voluntary.","q":"But an ascent to the gods imparts a smooth and gentle, instead of a hard and rough, and a voluntary, instead of a compelled life."},{"s":2030,"ri":354,"ph":"Leto is so called because of the meekness of the god","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":314,"ref":"","n":"Proclus rejects the reading of Leto as matter and Apollo as harmony, holding instead that Leto is the mother and fountain of all life-giving light.","q":"It is better, therefore, to say, that Latona is not the receptacle of Apollo, but that she is the mother and fountain of all vivific light, which preserves all things by heat:"},{"s":2030,"ri":354,"ph":"perhaps her name is Lētho","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":314,"ref":"","n":"Proclus suggests Socrates derives the name from Lethe because Leto gives souls forgetfulness of evils and the storms of generation.","q":"But you may say that Socrates derives her name from Lethe, because she peculiarly causes in souls an oblivion of evils, and of the roughness and storms in generation;"},{"s":2030,"ri":354,"ph":"her preference for virginity (parthenia)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":315,"ref":"","n":"Proclus finds three properties of Artemis in Plato, the undefiled, the cosmic and the anagogic; the first makes her a lover of virginity.","q":"That of our sovereign mistress Diana, Plato delivers three idioms; the undefiled, the mundane, and the anagogic. And through the first of these indeed the goddess is said to be a lover of virginity;"},{"s":2030,"ri":354,"ph":"'she who knows excellence' (aretēs histora)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":315,"ref":"","n":"Proclus takes this as the second, cosmic property: as perfecter of works Artemis is the overseeing guardian of virtue.","q":"but through the second, according to which she is perfective of works (τελεσιουργος), she is said to be the inspective guardian of virtue;"},{"s":2030,"ri":354,"ph":"her hatred of procreation (aroton misēsasēs)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":317,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says she spurns material sexual union while overseeing its fruits, being averse to the processes of generation yet bringing them to perfection.","q":"They say too, that she aspires after virginity, since the form of her is comprehended in the vivific fountain, and she understands fontal virtue, gives subsistence to supermundane and anagogic virtue, and despises all material sexual connexion, though she inspects the fruits arising from it."},{"s":2031,"ri":355,"ph":"What about Dionysus and Aphrodite?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":318,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains why Plato pairs the cosmic Dionysus with the cosmic Aphrodite: her love for Dionysus led her to fashion Adonis as his image.","q":"That Plato coarranges the mundane Bacchus with the mundane Venus, in consequence of her love of Bacchus, and her fashioning, as an image of him, Adonis much honoured in Cilicia and Cyprus."},{"s":2031,"ri":356,"ph":"you are asking me a big question","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":319,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says the young man asks about Dionysus as if it were trivial, and for this reason Socrates checks him.","q":"That the young man appears to inquire about our sovereign master Bacchus, as if it were about things of small importance, and on this account he is silenced by Socrates."},{"s":2031,"ri":356,"ph":"a serious way of explaining the names of these gods, and a playful one","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":319,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Hermogenes hears only of the last, cosmic processions of these gods, which the wise man still reveres even though he calls them play.","q":"And he does not indeed hear concerning the occult, but only the last and mundane progressions of the gods. These indeed the wise man venerates, though, as he says, they are sports, through these gods being lovers of sport."},{"s":2031,"ri":356,"ph":"the gods are quite fond of playing","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":319,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains why these gods love play: their gifts strengthen the weakness of mortal nature and relieve bodily distress.","q":"For, because the terminations of these divinities strengthen the infirmity of the mortal nature, and recall corporeal molestation, on this account the gods, the causes of these things, are φιλοπαιγμονες, lovers of sport ."},{"s":2032,"ri":356,"ph":"being playfully called 'Didoinysos'","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":323,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says theologians call Dionysus wine after his last gift, so Socrates rightly begins the name from wine, which reveals all the god's powers.","q":"so that Socrates, now looking to this, calls the god διδοινυσος, beginning from wine, which, as we have said, manifests all the powers of the god."},{"s":2032,"ri":356,"ph":"it may correctly be called 'oionous'","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":323,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reads oinos as oionous, a particular intellect, form separated from the whole intellect and participated, solar or lunar for example.","q":"This therefore, the idiom of which is to be separated from the rest, wine indicates, signifying an intellect such as , and particular (σημαινων τον οιον και τινα νουν)."},{"s":2032,"ri":356,"ph":"believe (oieithai) that they are possessed of intelligence (nous) when they are not","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":323,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains the false belief: in bodies wine acts as an image, through false opinion and imagination, but in intellectual natures as true intellect and fabrication.","q":"Wine therefore energizes in things analogous to its subsistence in them: in body, indeed, after the manner of an image, according to a false opinion and imagination; but in intellectual natures, according to an intellectual energy and fabrication;"},{"s":2032,"ri":356,"ph":"her birth from foam (afrou)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":324,"ref":"","n":"Proclus reports the material reading of the many, that foam is seed and Aphrodite the pleasure of intercourse, and sets out to give the divine conception instead.","q":"Thus, for instance, according to the material conceptions of the multitude, Venus derives her origin from foam; and foam corresponds to seed."},{"s":2032,"ri":356,"ph":"There is no need to contradict Hesiod about Aphrodite","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":325,"ref":"","n":"Proclus distinguishes two Aphrodites, both born from foam: the elder from the foam of Heaven's parts cast into the sea, the younger from Zeus with Dione.","q":"From the foam therefore of his own prolific parts thrown into the sea, Heaven produced this goddess, as Orpheus says."},{"s":2032,"ri":356,"ph":"because of her birth from foam (afrou)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":325,"ref":"","n":"Proclus interprets the image: the sea is extended life, its depth the all-reaching procession of that life, and its foam the purest and highest flower of nature.","q":"But the sea signifies an expanded and circumscribed life; its profundity, the universally extended progression of such a life; and its foam, the greatest purity of nature, that which is full of prolific light and power, and that which swims upon all life, and is as it were its highest flower."},{"s":2033,"ri":357,"ph":"Athena, or indeed Hephaestus or Ares","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":125,"ref":"","n":"Proclus explains why Plato groups these three: they share warlike concerns, Minerva has an art kindred to each of the others, both others love Venus, and all were born of Juno and Jupiter.","q":"That Plato coarranges Minerva, Vulcan, and Mars, through that which is common with respect to warlike concerns"},{"s":2033,"ri":362,"ph":"We do call her 'Pallas', I presume?","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":326,"ref":"","n":"Proclus says Socrates praises Athena's guarding power through the name Pallas, and her perfecting power through the name Athena.","q":"Now therefore Socrates celebrates her guardian power, through the name of Pallas ; but her perfec- tive power through that of Minerva ."},{"s":2034,"ri":364,"ph":"her dancing in armour","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":326,"ref":"","n":"Proclus links the armed dance to Athena as leader of the Curetes, armed with empyrean weapons to repress disorder and unfold dancing through rhythmic motion.","q":"For Minerva, according to this power, is the leader of the Curetes, as Orpheus says, whence also, as well as those divinities, she is adorned with empyrean arms, through which she represses all disorder, preserves the demiurgic series immovable, and unfolds dancing through rhythmical motion."},{"s":2035,"ri":370,"ph":"the intellect (noesis) of God (theou)","c":"Proclus","e":"late-antiquity","y":450,"w":"Scholia on the Cratylus (extracts, tr. Taylor)","k":"taylor-plato-notes-2","p":326,"ref":"","n":"Proclus names the two powers theologians celebrate in Athena: defensive, keeping the cosmic order unconquered by matter, and perfective, filling all things with intellectual light.","q":"That theologists especially celebrate two powers of our sovereign mistress Minerva, the defensive and the perfective , the former preserving the order of wholes undefiled, and unvanquished by matter, and the other filling all things with intellectual light, and converting them to their cause;"},{"s":2056,"ri":432,"ph":"who get dizzy because they perform so many about-turns","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":223,"ref":"","n":"Taylor says that in this middle part Plato facetiously ridicules the Heracliteans, who held all things to be perpetually flowing without any periods of repose.","q":"and in the middle part he facetiously ridicules the followers of Heraclitus, who considered all things as perpetually flowing, without admitting any periods of repose."},{"s":2057,"ri":434,"ph":"applied as if the things named are moving, flowing, and changing in every way","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":223,"ref":"","n":"Taylor holds that Plato deliberately abuses etymology here, showing that all names can be made to signify flowing things, in order to explode the flux doctrine when extended to intelligible natures.","q":"Hence, in order to explode this opinion, which is erroneous in the extreme, when extended to intelligible as well as sensible natures, he proves that, by an abuse of etymologies, all names may be shown to have been established, as belonging to things borne along, flowing, and in continual generation."},{"s":2059,"ri":438,"ph":"Sound-mindedness (sōphrosunē) is the preservation (sōtēria) of wisdom","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":190,"ref":"","n":"Taylor explains this etymology: temperance preserves prudence because all truth is inherent in the soul, which finds it by looking into herself unless hindered by immoderate love of the body.","q":"Plato in the Cratylus explains the name of temperance, as signifying a certain safety and preservation of prudence. For he considered all truth as naturally inherent in the soul; and that, in consequence of this, the soul by profoundly looking into herself will discover every truth. She is however impeded from this conversion to herself, by an immoderate love of body and corporeal natures."},{"s":2198,"ri":784,"ph":"if it is always slipping away, is it possible to say, firstly, that it is this","c":"Thomas Taylor","e":"modern","y":1804,"w":"Introductions to the Dialogues of Plato","k":"taylor-plato-introductions-2","p":228,"ref":"","n":"Taylor takes Socrates to show that names cannot be natural in the Heraclitean sense, since flowing individuals cannot keep a fitting name for any considerable length of time.","q":"And again, he shows that neither can names consist from nature in the manner which the Heraclitics endeavour to support, on account of the gliding and fluxible nature of individuals, to which names can neither be conveniently assigned nor well adapted for any considerable period of time."}]});
